-- Passage rg7-theme-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('05118b28-a66c-5255-ad20-ee042c6532b9', 'rg7-theme-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Heron and the Measuring Reed$q$, $q$At the edge of a wide marsh lived a young heron named Tamsin who could not stop comparing herself to others. Each morning she took a long reed in her beak and went around measuring.

"The egret's neck is longer than mine," she told the frogs. "The kingfisher dives faster. The swan's feathers are whiter." Every measurement made her feel smaller, and soon she spent more time measuring than fishing.

One dry summer, the marsh began to shrink. The swan grew worried, because the water was too shallow for her to float. The kingfisher could not dive into mud. The egret hunted for hours and caught almost nothing.

Tamsin, hungry and tired, waded out to the last deep channel. She stood on her thin legs for a long time, perfectly still, until the fish forgot she was there. Then she struck. She caught one fish, then another, then enough to carry back to the other birds.

"How did you do it?" asked the egret.

Tamsin thought about the question. Her legs were longer than anyone's, so she could stand in deeper water. She could wait without moving when other birds grew restless. She had never measured these things, because she had only looked for what she lacked.

That evening she dropped the reed into the water and watched it float away.$q$, 'draft')
