-- Passage rg7-theme-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2f954d15-ec7a-5df8-b727-69a5cf23e2c3', 'rg7-theme-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Ferry Logbook$q$, $q$The history project was supposed to be about someone famous. Everyone in Ms. Lindqvist's class picked a president, an inventor, or an explorer. Kofi picked nobody, because he could not decide.

On Saturday, he helped his uncle clean out the storage room at the town museum, where his uncle volunteered. In a dusty box with no label, Kofi found a cracked leather book. It was a logbook from the ferry that had crossed the Carver River before the bridge was built.

The pages were full of neat handwriting from a ferry pilot named Eliza Brandt. She had recorded every crossing for eleven years. Some entries were short: "Fog. Two wagons. One cow." Others were longer. During the flood of 1889, she wrote that she made fourteen trips in one night, carrying families and their belongings to higher ground.

Kofi read until his uncle turned off the lights. Nobody in any textbook had heard of Eliza Brandt. Yet without her, dozens of families might have lost everything.

He changed his project. His classmates' posters showed portraits and famous dates. Kofi's showed a map of the river, a copy of one page from the logbook, and a list of the families Eliza carried during the flood. At the bottom, he wrote a question: "Who kept your town running?"

After the presentations, three classmates asked if they could visit the museum storage room. One of them found her own great-grandfather's name in the flood list.$q$, 'draft')
